\section{Expose a Public Route Without Moving the Definition}

A chart library has separate implementation modules for rings,
axes, and grids. Its author wants consumers to write
\texttt{Charts.Grid}, not \texttt{Charts.Layout.Grid}. The first
name can be a re-export of the type defined in \texttt{Layout}.
An instance created through either route (where both routes are
visible) belongs to the same type. By contrast, a wrapper around
the type can impose a new invariant and a forwarding function can
provide a new calling contract. Neither is the same as re-exporting
the original definition. Rust's distinction between local
\texttt{use} and \texttt{pub use} is a concrete example of turning
a name binding into part of the downstream surface.

An export table for the language can map a pair of namespace and
public spelling to a canonical definition, an effective visibility,
and provenance. A linker export table \cite{levine2000linkers}
answers another question: which binary name can be bound from
outside this file or image? Re-exporting a source type need not add
a binary symbol; re-exporting a function does not by itself
require an extra binary entry point. A language may choose to
preserve a binary name as a separate ABI promise.

\section{Compute the Surface from Legal Routes}

Allow the owner of an item to authorize publication from a private
module without opening that module's entire contents. In our toy
language, \texttt{Layout.Grid} is marked re-exportable by its owner
but the \texttt{Layout} route is internal. The library publishes
the item under \texttt{Charts.Grid}; \texttt{Layout.pack} is not
marked re-exportable. A valid private import is therefore not
automatically a valid re-export. The builder checks the declaration
and each proposed route under its own access policy. In this
example \texttt{Grid} is public in the table of \texttt{Layout},
but the \texttt{Layout} module itself is not exported by
\texttt{Charts}. Local table membership alone does not make a
path from the package root public.
\Cref{fig:visual-399} distinguishes the public spelling from
the implementation path.

