\section{Propagate Candidates Before Reporting Conflicts}

\begin{verbatim}
build_export_tables(modules):
    for m in modules:
        explicit_keys[m] := declared_explicit_public_names(m)
        candidates[m] := checked_public_local_declarations(m)

    repeat until no candidate set grows:
        for (src, in_key, dst, out_key) in named_reexports:
            for item in candidates[src][in_key]:
                require_named_route_allowed(src, dst, item)
                add_candidate(dst, out_key, item.definition)

        for (src, dst) in wildcard_reexports:
            for (key, item) in candidates[src]:
                if key in explicit_keys[dst]: continue
                if not wildcard_route_allowed(src, dst, item): continue
                add_candidate(dst, key, item.definition)

    require_each_named_source_resolved_unambiguously()
    for (m, key) in occupied_keys(candidates):
        ids := candidates[m][key]
        if size(ids) != 1: error_with_routes(m, key, ids)
        exports[m][key] := only(ids)
    return exports
\end{verbatim}

This deliberately simple policy rejects ambiguity even at a
wildcard-generated export. A more permissive language may omit an
ambiguous name until an importer tries to use it. The keys for
explicit exports are reserved before propagation, so an explicit
export shadows wildcard results from the outset, even when it
cannot yet resolve. The set of valid identities per key is finite
and only grows. Propagating entire chains would not terminate on a
cycle; store provenance edges separately and reconstruct one or
two acyclic paths for diagnostics. A strongly connected component
containing only aliases and no defining origin cannot invent a
definition. Report an ungrounded named re-export rather than
treating the cycle as an item.

\section{Test the Facade as an External Consumer}

Exercise the public paths by compiling a fixture outside the
library, where private access is unavailable. Check that the
facade publishes the two expected routes if it is keeping a
deprecated spelling, and that both denote the same item. Move
the implementation and repeat, preserving canonical identity
where the language and ABI promise it. A wildcard may unintentionally
expand the facade when an implementation module adds a declaration.
Diff a normalized manifest of public names, signatures, and aliases
to catch unreviewed additions and removals. Include namespace in
the comparison: keeping a type but losing a same-spelled
constructor is still a breaking surface change.

A final fixture should import a diamond: two submodules publish
the same \texttt{Grid}, then the facade imports both. It should
succeed without a duplicate-type error. Change one arm to a
distinct \texttt{Grid}, require a conflict, and resolve it with an
explicit export. These tests distinguish duplicate reachability
from duplicate definition and make results insensitive to
the order in which modules happened to be visited.

\section{Distinguish a Facade Change from a Replacement Definition}

Suppose \texttt{Charts.Grid} was published before the package's
internal layout was split. In the next release, a new constructor
algorithm belongs in \texttt{Geometry}, but the old \texttt{Grid}
is embedded in values passed between libraries. Reassigning
\texttt{Charts.Grid} to a newly declared but otherwise identical
record changes its nominal identity. This matters if a provider
still returns the original type through a different public path:
equal field lists are not proof that the two values belong to the
same declaration. A correct alias preserves the existing identity;
an explicit adapter can convert if the package intentionally
replaces the type.

\begin{verbatim}
release 1: Charts.Grid -> id 17
           Layout.Grid -> id 17
release 2: Charts.Grid -> id 17
         Geometry.Grid -> id 17
replacement:
           Charts.Grid -> id 49
         Geometry.Grid -> id 17
\end{verbatim}

Treat these numbers as stable IDs scoped to a compatible package
line, not as IDs shared by arbitrary installed versions. A package
that derives every identity from its original module path needs
an explicit migration rule or a retained declaration to achieve the
second row. It must also decide what happens to the old public
\texttt{Layout.Grid} path. Removing it is a source break even
though every surviving route still points to the original item.
Keeping a deprecated alias lets a compiler diagnose the old path
while presenting a single canonical name in documentation.

Changes to a facade can carry behavior beyond the spelling.
Re-exporting an existing function normally does not change its
effects; a new forwarding function might log, translate errors,
or run at a different point in initialization. If the facade
needs to adapt behavior, use a wrapper and document that contract.
If its role is to keep names stable while internal code moves,
prefer the identity-preserving route. Test both public source
compatibility and binary compatibility if the package promises
both; a generated name manifest checks only the former.
